From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:12:04 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: qcom: Leave USB4 when the port falls back to
 plain USB

A USB4 partner can drop USB4 and enter USB 3 while it stays plugged in.
An ASMedia ASM2464PD NVMe enclosure does this ten seconds after it is
connected if no PCIe tunnel is set up to it, which is the case when it
is attached while the system boots: nothing authorises it before the
root file system is unlocked and bolt runs. The PD firmware then
reports the port as USB 3 only and the alternate mode driver sets the
plain USB state:

  thunderbolt 0-2: device disconnected
  pmic_glink_altmode: PAN port 1 orientation 0 version 1 mux 1 ...
  qcom-usb4-host 15800000.usb4: Type-C USB state, connection kept

The router's mux ignored that state, because the UCSI driver used to
set it for a partner operating in USB4 too. The router stayed started
with the PHY in USB4 mode, the USB controller never got the lanes, and
the drive was unusable until it was plugged in again.

With the UCSI driver no longer setting the state for a USB4 partner,
treat it like the safe state and a DisplayPort alternate mode: send the
router its disconnect command and let the idle router be stopped, which
hands the PHY back in the mode the partner asked for.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c | 19 +++++++------------
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c
index 367f8d0bc..cf331221b 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c
@@ -580,18 +580,6 @@ static int qcom_usb4_host_mux_set(struct typec_mux_dev *mux, struct typec_mux_st
 	int ret;
 
 	mutex_lock(&host->lock);
-	if (state->mode == TYPEC_STATE_USB && !state->alt) {
-		/*
-		 * The UCSI driver also sets the plain USB state on the port when
-		 * a partner registers; that is not a firmware mux change. Only the
-		 * alternate-mode driver's safe state or a DP alternate mode ends
-		 * a tunnel, so leave an established connection alone here.
-		 */
-		dev_dbg(host->dev, "Type-C USB state, connection %s\n",
-			host->connected ? "kept" : "absent");
-		mutex_unlock(&host->lock);
-		return 0;
-	}
 	if (!state->alt && state->mode == TYPEC_MODE_USB4 && state->data) {
 		ret = qcom_usb4_typec_usb4_command(state->data, host->orientation,
 						   QCOM_USB4_SURFACE_BOARD_RETIMER, &command);
@@ -602,6 +590,13 @@ static int qcom_usb4_host_mux_set(struct typec_mux_dev *mux, struct typec_mux_st
 		request = QCOM_USB4_LINK_CONNECT;
 		submode = PHY_SUBMODE_TBT3;
 	} else {
+		/*
+		 * Every other state ends a USB4 connection: the safe state, a
+		 * DisplayPort alternate mode, and plain USB, which a partner
+		 * falls back to by itself, for example an NVMe enclosure that
+		 * was given no PCIe tunnel. The port then belongs to the USB
+		 * controller again.
+		 */
 		ret = qcom_usb4_typec_disconnect_command(&command);
 	}
 	if (ret) {
-- 
